Honorable members of the Texas Board of Pardons and Parole, 

I am writing to you today on behalf of Jeremiah Zebulon Strickland, DOB 04/14/1979, SPN 03097160 in attestment to his character, in validation of his service to the community, and in advocacy of his competent ability to successfully compete parole according to the requirements determined by this Board. I thank you for the time and attention you have devoted to hearing from me and to deliberating the proper decision in regards to his parole. 

I am a board certified Nurse Anesthetist with active licenses and work history across the United States. I met Jeremiah Strickland on spiritual retreat in the mountains of California 4 and a half years ago and have spent almost every day with him since that time. 

Jeremiah has a heart of gold. He is a genuinely kind and respectful, God loving and Christ-led man. I have had the privledge of witnessing him minister to countless people in the last 4 years. I have watched him deal with siege after siege of adversity and trial with nobility and grace. I have seen the impact of his service to this country extend in ripples across the nation. The man does not rest or play when it comes to service. He is in continuous effort to add benefit, ingenuity, compassion, wisdom, support and blessing to whatever person, place or situation he encounters. He strives to leave any situation better than when he entered it. And he is extremely successful in accomplishing this goal. 

I understand he has been though some extremely trying circumstances with his own family. It was indeed because of this that God called us together. The man gives selflessly, endlessly, as is the nature of one living in continuous renewal of the Spirit. In this circumstance with his personal family he found himself in need of receiving some support, something he is unfamiliar with needing or receiving very often. 

While the circumstances and nature of what occured in the trials with his family are not mine to convey to you, I believe he has written some explanation of what happened in his Parole Review Packet. It is only my place to say that he has received the assistance he needed to learn from, heal and grow from this circumstance. He is a changed man. He was a good man before and caught caught up in some dark troubles, not of his own accord. He acted out of character due to the severe nature of the circumstances. He has learned and grown into a greater man because of it. And he offers back only hope, restitution and inspiration to all parties involved. 

While many are blissfully unaware of the depths of spiritual warfare going on in this country, others have been deeply affected and wounded. Jeremiah has royally triumphed in this arena and has much to offer in the way of support, encouragement and healing to other veterans of these battles. I boldly stand on the certainty that every thing he has traversed which has brought him before your Board for review is directly related to these battles and to his victory. 

Jeremiah is a parishoner at St. Michael's the Archangel Catholic Church here in Houston, Texas. He is extremely active in the Catholic, Christian and Jewish communities, serving the needs of their respective congregations. He is silently active in Hindu, Taoist and Buddhist communities, in full respect and honor to their mode of service and worship of the Divine. His heart holds a special place for the Native American traditions and a reverance for nature that many never have the privilege of seeing in him. Reverence is not for show.

He also has been active in the Trump administration campaign and advocates for human rights, protection of life and liberty, and restoring our nation to true greatness. He holds the family unit as sacred and the staring point of true reformation. 

Jeremiah rarely speaks on his own behalf. This is in part because he has disciplined himself to accept life's circumstances and challenges with courage, optimism and creativity. And it is in part because of his full faith and reliance on God to meet his needs. In times like this God uses people like me, and like all of you, to support His Plans of freedom, redemption and leadership for this man, a chosen son of God. 


Jeremiah is a devoted family man. He is a loyal countyman. He is simply a good man. 

He poses no threat or risk to society except to say he is an exacter of great change and where society can benefit from this, he willing contributes his influence, which does disrupt the old to make room for the new. Where this at times causes discomfort in some, that is to be taken up with The Most High, which is exactly what God intends for us. God is calling all of us, all the time, towards the longing and orientation of our souls. In truth, there is no greater service that Jeremiah could provide but to stir the human mind and spirit to break free of old conditions and patterns and seek the liberation in devoted service to the Soul.

Jeremiah has many skills in addition to his powerful spiritual gifts and leanings. And he has the moral character and discipline to support the proper utilization of these skills to implement many benefits to others. Most of all, he has the powerful desire to do so. 

I have created a company called MagaCOPS Ltd. as a vehicle of service to veterans of the  spiritual battlefield and named Jeremiah as the registered agent. Upon his release he has a management position waiting for him to put to use his skills and experience. He is being offered full creative leniency, and this decision is well founded. 

I could write pages in great confidence and support of Jeremiah's ability and certainty of success if offered the opportunity to parole out on his sentence. Suffice to say, he is more than eligible because of time served. He is eligible because of his upright character, his unyielding drive, and his unparalleled devotion to a life of service. 

It is with a full heart I bessech the Board Members involved in his Parole Review to find in his favor. 

Thank you for taking the time to listen and having the heart to care. I am certain you would not be here today if it were not so. Thank you each for your time and your service. 

Respectfully,

Heather N. Franasiak 



